## Solution

**Key identity.** Recall the arctangent subtraction formula (valid when $ab > -1$):
$$\tan^{-1}(a) - \tan^{-1}(b) = \tan^{-1}\!\left(\frac{a-b}{1+ab}\right).$$

**Telescoping decomposition.** Set $a_n = \tfrac{2}{n}$ and $b_n = \tfrac{2}{n+1}$. Then
$$\frac{a_n - b_n}{1 + a_n b_n} = \frac{\frac{2}{n} - \frac{2}{n+1}}{1 + \frac{4}{n(n+1)}} = \frac{\frac{2}{n(n+1)}}{\frac{n(n+1)+4}{n(n+1)}} = \frac{2}{n^2+n+4}.$$

Since $a_n, b_n > 0$ (so $a_n b_n > -1$), the identity applies and gives
$$\tan^{-1}\!\left(\frac{2}{n^2+n+4}\right) = \tan^{-1}\!\left(\frac{2}{n}\right) - \tan^{-1}\!\left(\frac{2}{n+1}\right).$$

**Telescoping sum.** The partial sum from $n=1$ to $N$ is
$$\sum_{n=1}^{N}\left[\tan^{-1}\!\left(\frac{2}{n}\right) - \tan^{-1}\!\left(\frac{2}{n+1}\right)\right] = \tan^{-1}(2) - \tan^{-1}\!\left(\frac{2}{N+1}\right).$$

Taking $N \to \infty$, since $\tan^{-1}\!\left(\frac{2}{N+1}\right) \to 0$:
$$\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \tan^{-1}\!\left(\frac{2}{n^2+n+4}\right) = \tan^{-1}(2).$$

**Matching the right-hand side.** We have
$$\tan^{-1}(2) = \tan^{-1}\!\left(\frac{\alpha}{5}\right),$$
so $\frac{\alpha}{5} = 2$, giving
$$\boxed{\alpha = 10}.$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
